# IDOR - THÊM CHI TIẾT
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

add(
    "What are the three ingredients that create an IDOR vulnerability?",
    "According to OWASP, an IDOR vulnerability requires three specific ingredients: 1) An OBJECT - such as an account, document, support ticket, transaction, partner profile, order, invoice, or any database record. 2) A REFERENCE to that object in the form of an ID, UUID, account number, token, filename, slug, or other identifier. This reference is typically exposed in URLs, hidden form fields, cookies, API parameters, or HTTP headers. 3) A MISSING OBJECT-LEVEL AUTHORIZATION CHECK - the server does not verify whether the currently authenticated user should be allowed to access the specific object being referenced. Without this check, the server trusts the client-supplied reference blindly. For example: GET /users/123/profile returns user 123's profile. The server checks if the requester is authenticated (session valid) but doesn't check if the requester IS user 123. Attacker changes to GET /users/456/profile and accesses another user's data. The third ingredient - missing authorization check - is what makes it a vulnerability.",
    "idor", "medium", "A01-IDOR-101"
)

add(
    "Give me detailed IDOR attack examples in different contexts.",
    "Here are detailed IDOR examples across different application contexts:\n\n1. E-COMMERCE ORDER ACCESS: Normal request GET /api/orders/ORD-10023 returns user's own order. Attack: GET /api/orders/ORD-10024 with same session returns another customer's order with payment info and shipping addresses.\n\n2. DOCUMENT MANAGEMENT: Normal: GET /documents/annual-report.pdf. Attack: GET /documents/salary-data.pdf or /documents/merger-plans.pdf to access confidential business documents.\n\n3. BANKING TRANSFERS: Normal POST /api/transfers with from_account=10023 transfers from own account. Attack: POST with from_account=10024 transfers from another user's account to attacker's account.\n\n4. ADMIN ACTIONS: Normal GET /api/users/10023 lets regular user see their profile. Attack: GET /api/users/10001 accesses admin user data.\n\n5. FILE OPERATIONS: Normal DELETE /api/files/file-abc123 deletes user's own file. Attack: DELETE /api/files/file-xyz789 destroys another user's file.\n\n6. PASSWORD RESET: Normal POST /api/reset-password with user_id=10023 resets own password. Attack: POST with user_id=10001 resets admin's password if token not user-bound.\n\nThe common pattern: the attacker provides a different object identifier than the one they should be authorized to access, and the server processes the request without verifying authorization for that specific object.",
    "idor", "hard", "A01-IDOR-102"
)

add(
    "How do I prevent IDOR in Ruby on Rails?",
    "IDOR prevention in Ruby on Rails follows the principle of filtering queries by the current user:\n\nVULNERABLE CODE:\n```ruby\n# Searches ALL projects, regardless of owner\n@project = Project.find(params[:id])\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 1 - Scope to current user:\n```ruby\n# Only searches current_user's projects\n@project = current_user.projects.find(params[:id])\n# Raises ActiveRecord::RecordNotFound if user doesn't own it\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 2 - Use Pundit authorization gem:\n```ruby\n# In ProjectPolicy\nclass ProjectPolicy < ApplicationPolicy\n  def show?\n    record.user_id == user.id || user.admin?\n  end\n  \n  def update?\n    record.user_id == user.id\n  end\nend\n\n# In controller\nclass ProjectsController < ApplicationController\n  def show\n    @project = Project.find(params[:id])\n    authorize @project  # Pundit checks authorization\n  end\nend\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 3 - Use CanCanCan:\n```ruby\n# In Ability model\nclass Ability\n  include CanCan::Ability\n  \n  def initialize(user)\n    can :read, Project, user_id: user.id\n    can :update, Project, user_id: user.id\n    can :manage, Project if user.admin?\n  end\nend\n\n# In controller\nclass ProjectsController < ApplicationController\n  load_and_authorize_resource  # Auto-checks authorization\nend\n```\n\nKEY BEST PRACTICES:\n1. Always use ActiveRecord associations that scope to current user\n2. Implement authorization gems (Pundit, CanCanCan, Action Policy)\n3. Test with multiple user accounts during development\n4. Include ownership checks in all CRUD operations\n5. Use UUIDs instead of sequential IDs for defense-in-depth\n6. Never expose internal primary keys directly in URLs",
    "idor", "hard", "A01-IDOR-103"
)

add(
    "How do I prevent IDOR in Django?",
    "IDOR prevention in Django uses QuerySet filtering and permission systems:\n\nVULNERABLE CODE:\n```python\n# Returns ANY project by ID\nproject = Project.objects.get(id=request.GET['id'])\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 1 - Filter by owner:\n```python\n# Only returns project if current user owns it\nproject = Project.objects.get(\n    id=request.GET['id'],\n    owner=request.user\n)\n# Raises DoesNotExist if user doesn't own it\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 2 - Use get_object_or_404:\n```python\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404\n\n# Returns 404 if not found or not owned by user\nproject = get_object_or_404(\n    Project,\n    id=pk,\n    owner=request.user\n)\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 3 - Use Django Guardian for object-level permissions:\n```python\n# settings.py\nINSTALLED_APPS = ['guardian', ...]\nAUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS = (\n    'django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend',\n    'guardian.backends.ObjectPermissionBackend',\n)\n\n# In views\nfrom guardian.shortcuts import get_objects_for_user, assign_perm\n\n# Grant permission\nassign_perm('view_project', user, project)\n\n# Query only permitted objects\nprojects = get_objects_for_user(\n    request.user,\n    'view_project',\n    Project\n)\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 4 - Use Django REST Framework permissions:\n```python\nfrom rest_framework import permissions\n\nclass IsOwner(permissions.BasePermission):\n    def has_object_permission(self, request, view, obj):\n        return obj.owner == request.user\n\nclass ProjectViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet):\n    permission_classes = [IsOwner]\n    \n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return Project.objects.filter(owner=self.request.user)\n```\n\nKEY PRACTICES:\n1. Always filter QuerySets by current user\n2. Use get_object_or_404 with owner filter\n3. Implement Django Guardian for fine-grained permissions\n4. Use DRF permission classes for APIs\n5. Test with multiple user accounts\n6. Include ownership in all CRUD operations",
    "idor", "hard", "A01-IDOR-104"
)

add(
    "How do I prevent IDOR in Spring Boot Java?",
    "IDOR prevention in Spring Boot uses Spring Security's method-level security:\n\nVULNERABLE CODE:\n```java\n@GetMapping(\"/projects/{id}\")\npublic Project getProject(@PathVariable Long id) {\n    return projectRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n}\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 1 - Use @PreAuthorize:\n```java\n@Service\npublic class ProjectSecurity {\n    public boolean isOwner(Long projectId, String username) {\n        Project project = projectRepository.findById(projectId).orElse(null);\n        return project != null && project.getOwner().getUsername().equals(username);\n    }\n}\n\n@RestController\npublic class ProjectController {\n    @PreAuthorize(\"@projectSecurity.isOwner(#id, authentication.name)\")\n    @GetMapping(\"/projects/{id}\")\n    public Project getProject(@PathVariable Long id) {\n        return projectRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 2 - Filter in repository:\n```java\npublic interface ProjectRepository extends JpaRepository<Project, Long> {\n    Optional<Project> findByIdAndOwnerUsername(Long id, String username);\n}\n\n@RestController\npublic class ProjectController {\n    @GetMapping(\"/projects/{id}\")\n    public Project getProject(@PathVariable Long id, @AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails user) {\n        return projectRepository\n            .findByIdAndOwnerUsername(id, user.getUsername())\n            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 3 - Use Spring Security ACL:\n```java\n@Configuration\n@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)\npublic class SecurityConfig extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {\n    // Enables @PreAuthorize, @PostAuthorize annotations\n}\n\n// In controller\n@PreAuthorize(\"hasPermission(#id, 'Project', 'READ')\")\n@GetMapping(\"/projects/{id}\")\npublic Project getProject(@PathVariable Long id) {\n    return projectRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n}\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 4 - Custom annotation:\n```java\n@Target(ElementType.METHOD)\n@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)\n@PreAuthorize(\"@authService.isOwner(#id, authentication.name)\")\npublic @interface RequireOwnership {}\n\n// Usage\n@RequireOwnership\n@GetMapping(\"/projects/{id}\")\npublic Project getProject(@PathVariable Long id) {\n    return projectRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n}\n```\n\nKEY PRACTICES:\n1. Enable method-level security with @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity\n2. Use @PreAuthorize for declarative authorization\n3. Create repository methods that filter by owner\n4. Implement custom authorization services\n5. Use Spring Security ACL for complex scenarios\n6. Always verify ownership server-side",
    "idor", "hard", "A01-IDOR-105"
)

add(
    "How do I prevent IDOR in Express.js Node.js?",
    "IDOR prevention in Express.js uses middleware to verify ownership:\n\nVULNERABLE CODE:\n```javascript\napp.get('/api/projects/:id', (req, res) => {\n  Project.findById(req.params.id)\n    .then(project => res.json(project));\n});\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 1 - Custom middleware:\n```javascript\nconst requireOwnership = async (req, res, next) => {\n  try {\n    const project = await Project.findById(req.params.id);\n    \n    if (!project) {\n      return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });\n    }\n    \n    if (project.ownerId.toString() !== req.user.id) {\n      return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Not authorized' });\n    }\n    \n    req.project = project; // Attach for next handlers\n    next();\n  } catch (err) {\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Server error' });\n  }\n};\n\n// Usage\napp.get('/api/projects/:id', requireOwnership, (req, res) => {\n  res.json(req.project);\n});\n\napp.put('/api/projects/:id', requireOwnership, async (req, res) => {\n  req.project.name = req.body.name;\n  await req.project.save();\n  res.json(req.project);\n});\n\napp.delete('/api/projects/:id', requireOwnership, async (req, res) => {\n  await req.project.remove();\n  res.status(204).send();\n});\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 2 - Use CASL for authorization:\n```javascript\nconst { AbilityBuilder, Ability } = require('@casl/ability');\n\nfunction defineAbilitiesFor(user) {\n  const { can, rules } = new AbilityBuilder();\n  \n  // User can manage their own projects\n  can('manage', 'Project', { ownerId: user.id });\n  \n  // Admin can manage all projects\n  if (user.role === 'admin') {\n    can('manage', 'Project');\n  }\n  \n  return new Ability(rules);\n}\n\n// Middleware\nconst checkAbility = (action, subject) => {\n  return (req, res, next) => {\n    const ability = defineAbilitiesFor(req.user);\n    \n    if (ability.cannot(action, subject)) {\n      return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });\n    }\n    \n    next();\n  };\n};\n\n// Usage\napp.get('/api/projects/:id', async (req, res) => {\n  const project = await Project.findById(req.params.id);\n  \n  const ability = defineAbilitiesFor(req.user);\n  if (ability.cannot('read', project)) {\n    return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });\n  }\n  \n  res.json(project);\n});\n```\n\nSECURE APPROACH 3 - Query filtering:\n```javascript\napp.get('/api/projects/:id', async (req, res) => {\n  // Only find if current user owns it\n  const project = await Project.findOne({\n    _id: req.params.id,\n    ownerId: req.user.id\n  });\n  \n  if (!project) {\n    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });\n  }\n  \n  res.json(project);\n});\n```\n\nKEY PRACTICES:\n1. Always verify ownership in middleware before controller\n2. Use findOne with ownerId filter instead of findById\n3. Implement reusable middleware for ownership checks\n4. Use CASL or similar library for complex authorization\n5. Return 403 for unauthorized, 404 for not found\n6. Test with multiple user accounts",
    "idor", "hard", "A01-IDOR-106"
)

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PRIVILEGE ESCALATION
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

add(
    "What is vertical privilege escalation and give examples?",
    "Vertical privilege escalation occurs when a user gains access to functionality or data that requires higher privileges than their current role. The attacker moves UP the privilege hierarchy from a lower-privilege account to a higher-privilege one.\n\nExamples:\n1. REGULAR USER TO ADMIN:\n- User logs in as regular 'viewer' role\n- Modifies cookie: role=viewer → role=admin\n- Server trusts the cookie value without verification\n- User now has admin capabilities\n\n2. URL PARAMETER TAMPERING:\n- Normal: GET /profile?user_id=10023 (own profile)\n- Attack: GET /admin/users?user_id=10001 (admin panel)\n- If server doesn't check role for /admin endpoint, user accesses admin functions\n\n3. CLIENT-SIDE ROLE ENFORCEMENT BYPASS:\n- Frontend JavaScript: if (user.role === 'admin') { showAdminPanel(); }\n- Attacker bypasses UI, calls API directly: curl -H 'Cookie: session=abc' https://app.com/api/admin/users\n- Server doesn't verify role, returns admin data\n\n4. JWT ROLE CLAIM TAMPERING:\n- Normal JWT payload: {\"user_id\": 123, \"role\": \"user\"}\n- Attacker decodes, changes to: {\"user_id\": 123, \"role\": \"admin\"}\n- Re-encodes (without valid signature)\n- If server doesn't validate signature, user has admin role\n\n5. HTTP REQUEST SMUGGLING:\n- Attacker sends specially crafted request that bypasses access control at reverse proxy\n- Gains access to admin endpoints\n\n6. BUSINESS LOGIC FLAWS:\n- User creates account with 'upgrade to admin' feature disabled\n- Modifies request: POST /api/upgrade {\"to_role\": \"admin\"}\n- Server processes upgrade without proper authorization\n\n7. DIRECT OBJECT REFERENCE TO ADMIN RESOURCES:\n- User discovers admin endpoint: /api/internal/admin-dashboard\n- Accesses directly: GET /api/internal/admin-dashboard\n- No role check, returns sensitive admin data\n\nPREVENTION:\n1. Server-side role verification on every admin endpoint\n2. Never trust client-supplied role values\n3. Validate JWT signatures properly\n4. Use role-based middleware/decorators\n5. Implement deny-by-default for admin functions\n6. Log and alert on failed role checks\n7. Use framework authorization libraries",
    "privilege_escalation", "medium", "A01-VPE-101"
)

add(
    "What is horizontal privilege escalation and how does it differ from vertical?",
    "Horizontal privilege escalation occurs when a user accesses resources belonging to OTHER USERS AT THE SAME PRIVILEGE LEVEL. Unlike vertical escalation (moving up to admin), horizontal stays at the same level but accesses unauthorized peer data.\n\nKey differences:\n- VERTICAL: user → admin (changes privilege level)\n- HORIZONTAL: User A → User B's data (same privilege level, different user)\n\nExamples of horizontal privilege escalation:\n1. IDOR ON PEER USER DATA:\n- User A (regular user) accesses GET /api/users/123 (own profile)\n- User A changes to GET /api/users/456 (User B's profile)\n- Server returns User B's PII without authorization check\n- Both users have same role, but User A accessed User B's data\n\n2. ORDER ACCESS:\n- User A views own order: GET /api/orders/ORD-10023\n- User A accesses User B's order: GET /api/orders/ORD-10024\n- Sees User B's payment info, shipping address, order details\n- Same privilege level, different user's data\n\n3. EMAIL ENUMERATION:\n- User A accesses own messages: GET /api/messages?user=123\n- User A changes: GET /api/messages?user=456\n- Reads User B's private messages\n\n4. FILE SHARING BYPASS:\n- User A shares file with specific users: file-id shared with User C only\n- User B (not in share list) accesses: GET /files/abc123\n- Server doesn't verify User B is in share list\n\n5. API KEY LEAKAGE:\n- User A's API response includes other users' API keys in metadata\n- User A extracts User B's API key\n- Uses User B's API key to access User B's resources\n\nWhy horizontal is HARDER TO DETECT:\n1. Looks like legitimate authenticated activity\n2. User IS authenticated and authorized for the endpoint\n3. Just accessing different object (not different endpoint)\n4. Automated scanners often miss it\n5. Requires understanding of application's data relationships\n\nPREVENTION:\n1. Object-level authorization on EVERY data access\n2. Verify current user owns or has explicit access to requested object\n3. Use QuerySets filtered by current user (Rails: current_user.projects)\n4. Implement ownership checks in middleware\n5. Use indirect references (UUIDs mapped server-side)\n6. Log access to other users' data for audit\n7. Implement rate limiting on peer data access\n\nTESTING METHODOLOGY:\n1. Create two test accounts with same role\n2. Log in as User A, access own resources\n3. Log in as User B, access same endpoints with User A's resource IDs\n4. If User B sees User A's data, it's horizontal privilege escalation\n5. Test all CRUD operations: read, create, update, delete",
    "privilege_escalation", "medium", "A01-HPE-101"
)

add(
    "How do JWT attacks enable privilege escalation?",
    "JWT (JSON Web Token) attacks are a major vector for privilege escalation through metadata manipulation. OWASP specifically lists JWT tampering as a key A01:2025 attack vector.\n\nATTACK 1: ALGORITHM CONFUSION (RS256 → HS256)\nHow it works:\n1. Server signs JWT with RS256 (asymmetric - private key signs, public key verifies)\n2. Attacker decodes JWT: {\"alg\": \"RS256\", \"role\": \"user\"}\n3. Attacker changes algorithm: {\"alg\": \"HS256\", \"role\": \"admin\"}\n4. Attacker signs with SERVER'S PUBLIC KEY as HMAC secret\n5. Server receives JWT, sees \"HS256\", verifies with public key (which it has for RS256)\n6. Signature validates, server accepts forged \"admin\" role\n\nPrevention: Enforce algorithm allowlist server-side, never accept client-specified algorithm\n\nATTACK 2: 'NONE' ALGORITHM\nHow it works:\n1. Attacker decodes JWT\n2. Changes to: {\"alg\": \"none\", \"role\": \"admin\"}\n3. Removes signature entirely\n4. Some libraries accept \"none\" as valid algorithm\n5. Server doesn't verify signature\n\nPrevention: Reject \"none\" algorithm explicitly, use libraries that don't support it\n\nATTACK 3: PAYLOAD MODIFICATION (NO SIGNATURE VALIDATION)\nHow it works:\n1. Server doesn't validate JWT signature at all\n2. Attacker decodes payload (base64), changes role claim\n3. Re-encodes, sends to server\n4. Server accepts modified payload\n5. Attacker has admin privileges\n\nPrevention: ALWAYS validate signature with trusted key\n\nATTACK 4: KEY CONFUSION (PUBLIC KEY AS HMAC SECRET)\nHow it works:\n1. Attacker obtains server's RSA public key (often exposed via JWKS endpoint)\n2. Changes algorithm to HS256\n3. Signs JWT using public key as HMAC secret\n4. Server verifies with public key, signature matches\n\nPrevention: Separate key management for symmetric vs asymmetric algorithms\n\nATTACK 5: JWK HEADER INJECTION\nHow it works:\n1. JWT header includes \"jwk\" field with attacker's public key\n2. Server uses this embedded key for verification\n3. Attacker signs with own private key\n4. Server verifies with attacker's embedded public key\n\nPrevention: Ignore \"jwk\" header, use server-side key store\n\nATTACK 6: TOKEN REPLAY\nHow it works:\n1. Attacker captures valid admin JWT before expiration\n2. Reuses token from different IP/location\n3. Server accepts token\n4. Attacker has admin access\n\nPrevention: Bind tokens to client fingerprint, implement token revocation, use short-lived tokens\n\nGENERAL PREVENTION:\n1. Use well-tested JWT libraries (PyJWT, jsonwebtoken)\n2. Always validate signature\n3. Enforce algorithm allowlist (RS256, ES256)\n4. Reject \"none\" algorithm\n5. Use short-lived tokens (15 minutes access + refresh token)\n6. Implement token revocation/blacklisting\n7. Store sensitive data server-side, only user ID in JWT\n8. Validate issuer, audience, expiration claims",
    "metadata_manipulation", "hard", "A01-META-101"
)

