add(
    "What is path traversal (CWE-22) and how does it work?",
    "Path traversal (also called directory traversal, CWE-22) is a vulnerability allowing attackers to access files outside the intended web root by manipulating user-supplied input used to construct file paths. OWASP maps CWE-22, CWE-23 (Relative Path Traversal), and CWE-36 (Absolute Path Traversal) to A01:2025.\n\nHow it works:\n1. Application accepts user input as filename\n2. Application concatenates input with base directory\n3. Application reads/writes file without validating path\n4. Attacker supplies '../' sequences to traverse upward\n\nVULNERABLE CODE:\n```python\nfilename = request.GET['file']\nfile_path = f'/var/www/uploads/{filename}'\nwith open(file_path, 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\nreturn content\n```\n\nATTACK: file=../../../../etc/passwd\nRESULT: /var/www/uploads/../../../../etc/passwd → /etc/passwd\nIMPACT: Attacker reads system password file\n\nTARGET FILES:\n- /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow (Unix credentials)\n- /etc/hosts, /etc/resolv.conf (network config)\n- C:\\Windows\\System32\\drivers\\etc\\hosts (Windows)\n- Application source code (.git, .env)\n- Database files\n- Log files\n\nVARIANTS:\n- CWE-22: Path Traversal (general)\n- CWE-23: Relative Path Traversal (using '../')\n- CWE-36: Absolute Path Traversal (using '/etc/passwd' directly)\n- CWE-59: Improper Link Resolution (symlink following)\n- CWE-61: UNIX Symbolic Link Following\n- CWE-65: Windows Hard Link\n\nWhy it's an access control failure: Application grants access to files the user shouldn't reach. Proper access control restricts file access to intended directory only.",
    "path_traversal", "medium", "A01-PT-101"
)

add(
    "What techniques bypass path traversal filters?",
    "Attackers use many encoding techniques to bypass path traversal filters:\n\n1. BASIC TRAVERSAL: ../../etc/passwd or ..\\..\\Windows\\System32 (Windows)\n\n2. URL ENCODING: %2e%2e%2f%2e%2e%2f = ../ in URL encoding. Double encoding: %252e%252e%252f - filters see %2e, app decodes twice to ../\n\n3. UNICODE/OVERLONG UTF-8: ..%c0%af = ../ (overlong UTF-8), ..%ef%bc%8f = ../ (fullwidth slash)\n\n4. NULL BYTE INJECTION: ../../etc/passwd%00.jpg - null byte terminates string in C systems, app thinks it's image, OS reads password file\n\n5. NORMALIZATION BYPASS: ....// = ../ after normalization, /./././././etc/passwd = /etc/passwd\n\n6. ABSOLUTE PATH: /etc/passwd directly, no traversal needed\n\n7. DOUBLE DECODE: %252e%252e%252f → filter decodes to %2e%2e%2f → app decodes to ../\n\n8. WINDOWS-SPECIFIC: ..\\ (backslash), C:\\Windows\\System32\\config\\sam\n\n9. MIXED ENCODING: ..%2f (mixed raw and encoded)\n\n10. CASE VARIATIONS: ..%2F (uppercase F) vs ..%2f (lowercase)\n\nDEFENSE STRATEGIES:\n1. Avoid user input in file paths entirely (use indirect references)\n2. Canonicalize first, then validate\n3. In Python: os.path.realpath() resolves symlinks and traversals\n4. In Java: File.getCanonicalPath()\n5. Whitelist allowed characters (no dots, slashes)\n6. Check resolved path starts with allowed base directory\n7. Run service in chroot/jail\n\nCRITICAL: Never rely on simple string replacement of '../' - attackers bypass with encoding.",
    "path_traversal", "hard", "A01-PT-102"
)

add(
    "How do I prevent path traversal in Python securely?",
    "Here's a comprehensive guide to preventing path traversal in Python:\n\nBEST PRACTICE 1 - Indirect references (AVOID USER INPUT):\n```python\n# Map file IDs to actual paths server-side\nFILE_MAPPING = {\n    'report2024': '/var/www/reports/annual-2024.pdf',\n    'manual': '/var/www/docs/manual.pdf',\n}\n\ndef download_file(file_id):\n    if file_id not in FILE_MAPPING:\n        return None\n    return open(FILE_MAPPING[file_id], 'rb').read()\n```\n\nBEST PRACTICE 2 - Canonicalize then validate:\n```python\nimport os\n\nBASE_DIR = '/var/www/uploads'\n\ndef safe_read_file(user_filename):\n    # Reject dangerous characters\n    if any(c in user_filename for c in ['..', '/', '\\\\', '\\0']):\n        return None\n    \n    # Join with base directory\n    path = os.path.join(BASE_DIR, user_filename)\n    \n    # Canonicalize (resolves symlinks, traversals)\n    canonical_path = os.path.realpath(path)\n    \n    # Verify still under base directory\n    base_realpath = os.path.realpath(BASE_DIR)\n    if not canonical_path.startswith(base_realpath + os.sep) and canonical_path != base_realpath:\n        return None  # Path traversal attempt!\n    \n    # Now safe to read\n    with open(canonical_path, 'r') as f:\n        return f.read()\n```\n\nBEST PRACTICE 3 - Whitelist approach:\n```python\nimport re\n\nALLOWED_FILES = {'report2024.pdf', 'manual.pdf', 'terms.pdf'}\n\ndef download_file(filename):\n    # Strict validation\n    if not re.match(r'^[a-zA-Z0-9_\\-]+\\.[a-zA-Z0-9]+$', filename):\n        return None\n    \n    if filename not in ALLOWED_FILES:\n        return None\n    \n    path = os.path.join(BASE_DIR, filename)\n    return open(path, 'rb').read()\n```\n\nDJANGO EXAMPLE:\n```python\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse, Http404\nimport os\n\ndef download_file(request, file_id):\n    # Look up in database\n    try:\n        file_obj = UploadedFile.objects.get(id=file_id, owner=request.user)\n    except UploadedFile.DoesNotExist:\n        raise Http404\n    \n    # Serve file\n    with open(file_obj.path, 'rb') as f:\n        response = HttpResponse(f.read(), content_type='application/octet-stream')\n        response['Content-Disposition'] = f'attachment; filename=\"{file_obj.name}\"'\n        return response\n```\n\nADDITIONAL DEFENSES:\n- Use pathlib.Path with resolve() for modern Python\n- Run web server in chroot\n- Use OS-level sandboxing (AppArmor, SELinux)\n- Monitor file access logs\n- Test with path traversal payloads in CI/CD\n\nTESTING:\n```python\n# Test cases\ntest_cases = [\n    '../etc/passwd',\n    '%2e%2e%2f%2e%2e%2fetc/passwd',\n    '/etc/passwd',\n    '....//....//etc/passwd',\n    'valid.txt',  # should work\n]\n\nfor test in test_cases:\n    result = safe_read_file(test)\n    print(f'{test}: {\"VULNERABLE\" if result else \"BLOCKED\"}')\n```",
    "path_traversal", "hard", "A01-PT-103"
)

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CSRF & SSRF
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

add(
    "What is CSRF and how does it relate to Broken Access Control?",
    "CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery, CWE-352) tricks an authenticated user into submitting requests to a web application where they're already authenticated. OWASP maps CWE-352 to A01:2025 Broken Access Control.\n\nHow CSRF works:\n1. Victim logs into bank.com (session cookie set)\n2. Victim visits evil.com\n3. evil.com contains: <img src='https://bank.com/transfer?to=attacker&amount=10000'>\n4. Browser automatically includes victim's session cookie\n5. Bank processes transfer as if victim authorized it\n6. Victim didn't authorize transfer, but app executed it\n\nWhy it's an access control failure: The application performs an action on behalf of the user without verifying the user actually intended that action. The access control (session validation) passes, but the user didn't authorize this specific action.\n\nCSRF ATTACK VECTORS:\n1. GET requests: <img src='https://target.com/delete?id=123'>\n2. POST requests: Auto-submitting form via JavaScript\n3. PUT/DELETE: Via XMLHttpRequest from malicious site\n4. JSON payloads: With custom headers via Flash or Java (older browsers)\n\nPREVENTION METHODS:\n1. ANTI-CSRF TOKENS:\n- Generate unique, unpredictable token per session or request\n- Include in forms/headers\n- Server validates on submission\n- Tokens must be tied to user session\n\n2. SAMESITE COOKIE ATTRIBUTE:\n```http\nSet-Cookie: session=abc123; SameSite=Strict\n```\n- SameSite=Strict: Cookie never sent cross-site\n- SameSite=Lax: Cookie sent on top-level GET navigation only\n- Prevents cross-site requests from including cookie\n\n3. VERIFY ORIGIN/REFERER:\n```python\n# Server-side check\nexpected_origin = 'https://app.com'\nif request.headers.get('Origin') != expected_origin:\n    return 403\n```\n\n4. CUSTOM REQUEST HEADERS:\n- Require X-Requested-With header\n- JavaScript can set it, cross-origin forms can't\n- CORS preflight blocks cross-origin custom headers\n\n5. RE-AUTHENTICATION:\n- Require password or 2FA for sensitive actions\n- Transfers, password changes, profile updates\n\n6. NEVER USE GET FOR STATE-CHANGING:\n- GET should be idempotent\n- POST/PUT/DELETE for mutations\n- CSRF attacks via GET are easiest\n\nFRAMEWORK EXAMPLES:\nDjango: @csrf_protect decorator, {% csrf_token %} template tag\nRails: protect_from_forgery in ApplicationController\nSpring: CsrfFilter, CsrfToken\nExpress: csurf middleware\n\nTESTING:\n1. Remove CSRF token from form\n2. Submit form from different domain\n3. Check if request succeeds without token validation\n4. Test with SameSite=None cookies",
    "csrf_ssrf", "medium", "A01-CSRF-101"
)

add(
    "What is SSRF and how can it be used to attack cloud environments?",
    "SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery, CWE-918) occurs when an attacker can make the server send requests to unintended locations. OWASP maps CWE-918 to A01:2025 Broken Access Control.\n\nHow SSRF works:\n1. Application has feature that fetches URLs (webhook, image preview, URL shortener)\n2. Attacker controls the URL parameter\n3. Server fetches the attacker-controlled URL\n4. Attacker accesses internal resources, cloud metadata, or external targets\n\nVULNERABLE CODE:\n```python\n@app.route('/preview')\ndef preview():\n    url = request.args.get('url')\n    response = requests.get(url)  # No validation!\n    return response.text\n```\n\nATTACK: /preview?url=http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/\nRESULT: Server fetches AWS EC2 metadata endpoint, returns IAM credentials\n\nCLOUD METADATA ATTACKS:\n1. AWS EC2: http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/\n   - /iam/security-credentials/ → IAM role credentials\n   - /instance-id, /hostname, /public-keys\n   \n2. GCP: http://metadata.google.internal/computeMetadata/v1/\n   - /instance/service-accounts/default/token → OAuth token\n   \n3. Azure: http://169.254.169.254/metadata/instance?api-version=2021-02-01\n   - Subscription ID, resource group, managed identity token\n\nCAPITAL ONE BREACH (2019):\n- SSRF via WAF misconfiguration\n- Attacker accessed EC2 metadata\n- Stole IAM credentials\n- Accessed S3 buckets with 100M+ customer records\n- $80M fine\n\nOTHER SSRF TARGETS:\n1. Internal services: http://localhost:6379 (Redis), http://10.0.0.1:8080\n2. File protocol: file:///etc/passwd (reads local files)\n3. DNS rebinding: Domain resolves to internal IP during request\n4. Redirect: Allowed domain redirects to internal resource\n5. Gopher/Dict protocols: gopher://, dict:// for service interaction\n\nPREVENTION:\n1. ALLOWLIST DOMAINS:\n```python\nALLOWED_DOMAINS = {'api.example.com', 'images.cdn.com'}\n\ndef is_safe_url(url):\n    parsed = urlparse(url)\n    return parsed.hostname in ALLOWED_DOMAINS\n```\n\n2. BLOCK INTERNAL IPs:\n```python\nimport ipaddress\n\ndef is_internal_ip(hostname):\n    try:\n        ip = socket.gethostbyname(hostname)\n        ip_obj = ipaddress.ip_address(ip)\n        return (\n            ip_obj.is_private or\n            ip_obj.is_loopback or\n            ip_obj.is_link_local or\n            ip_obj.is_reserved\n        )\n    except:\n        return True  # Block on error\n```\n\n3. BLOCK NON-HTTP SCHEMES:\n- Reject file://, gopher://, dict://, ftp://\n- Only allow http:// and https://\n\n4. DISABLE REDIRECTS:\n```python\nresponse = requests.get(url, allow_redirects=False)\n```\n\n5. NETWORK SEGMENTATION:\n- Run URL fetcher in isolated network\n- No access to internal services\n- No access to cloud metadata endpoint\n\n6. CLOUD METADATA PROTECTION:\n- AWS: Use IMDSv2 (requires token)\n- Block metadata endpoint at network level\n- Use IAM roles with minimal permissions\n\n7. RESPONSE VALIDATION:\n- Don't return raw response to user\n- Parse and sanitize response content",
    "csrf_ssrf", "hard", "A01-SSRF-101"
)

