add(
    "What tools can I use to test for Broken Access Control?",
    "Here are the essential tools for testing Broken Access Control vulnerabilities:\n\n1. BURP SUITE PROFESSIONAL (GOLD STANDARD):\n- Autorize extension: Automated IDOR testing with two user accounts\n- Intruder: Bulk enumeration of object IDs, parameter fuzzing\n- Repeater: Manual request manipulation\n- Scanner: Active scanning for access control issues\n- Proxy: Intercept and modify requests\n\n2. OWASP ZAP (FREE, OPEN-SOURCE):\n- Active scanner with authenticated scanning\n- ZAP API for automation\n- AJAX Spider for JavaScript-heavy apps\n- Fuzzer add-on for brute-forcing\n- Access Control Testing add-on\n\n3. NUCLEI (TEMPLATE-BASED):\n- Fast, scalable scanning\n- IDOR-specific templates\n- Access control templates\n- Run: nuclei -t access-control-templates.yaml -l urls.txt\n- Great for large scope\n\n4. POSTMAN/COLLECTION RUNNER:\n- Manual API testing\n- Collection variables for user switching\n- Automated test scripts\n- Environment switching between users\n\n5. CUSTOM PYTHON SCRIPTS:\n```python\nimport requests\n\n# Test IDOR across multiple accounts\ndef test_idor(base_url, user_a_token, user_b_token, resource_id):\n    # User A's view\n    r1 = requests.get(f'{base_url}/api/resource/{resource_id}',\n                      headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {user_a_token}'})\n    \n    # User B's view\n    r2 = requests.get(f'{base_url}/api/resource/{resource_id}',\n                      headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {user_b_token}'})\n    \n    # Compare responses\n    if r1.status_code == 200 and r2.status_code == 200:\n        if r1.json() == r2.json():\n            print(f'IDOR: Both users see same data for {resource_id}')\n```\n\n6. CURL (QUICK TESTING):\n```bash\n# Test access control\ncurl -H 'Cookie: session=abc' https://app.com/api/admin\ncurl -H 'Cookie: session=xyz' https://app.com/api/admin\n\n# Test with different users\ncurl -u user1:pass1 https://app.com/api/users/123\ncurl -u user2:pass2 https://app.com/api/users/123\n```\n\n7. NIKTO:\n- Checks for directory listing\n- Default files (.git, backups)\n- Misconfigurations\n- Run: nikto -h https://target.com\n\n8. DIRB/GOBUSTER:\n- Directory brute-forcing\n- File discovery\n- Run: gobuster dir -u https://target.com -w wordlist.txt\n\n9. CORS TESTING:\n- corsy: Automated CORS testing\n- CORScanner: CORS misconfiguration scanner\n- Manual testing with Burp\n\n10. AUTOMATED FRAMEWORKS:\n- Arachni: Ruby-based web scanner\n- Wapiti: Python-based scanner\n- Skipfish: Google's scanner\n\nTESTING METHODOLOGY:\n1. Map application endpoints and methods\n2. Create test accounts with different privilege levels\n3. Test each endpoint with each account\n4. Check for horizontal privilege escalation (peer access)\n5. Check for vertical privilege escalation (admin access)\n6. Test IDOR on all object references\n7. Test CORS configuration\n8. Test CSRF protection\n9. Test path traversal on file operations\n10. Document findings with proof of concept",
    "testing_tools", "medium", "A01-TOOL-101"
)

add(
    "How do I use Burp Suite Autorize for IDOR testing?",
    "Burp Suite Autorize is the most efficient tool for manual IDOR testing. Here's the complete workflow:\n\nSETUP:\n1. Install Autorize extension:\n   - Burp Suite → Extender → BApp Store\n   - Search \"Autorize\" and install\n   - Restart Burp if needed\n\n2. Configure browser profiles:\n   - Browser Profile 1: Log in as User A (regular user)\n   - Browser Profile 2: Log in as User B (another regular user)\n   - Use different browsers or incognito windows\n\n3. Configure Autorize:\n   - Open Autorize tab in Burp\n   - Set User A as \"Authorized user\"\n   - Set User B as \"Unauthorized user\"\n   - Configure enforcement detector (how to identify success/failure)\n\nTESTING WORKFLOW:\n1. Browse application as User A:\n   - Click through all features\n   - Access all resources User A should see\n   - Every request is captured by Burp\n\n2. Autorize automatically:\n   - Replays each User A request as User B\n   - Compares responses\n   - Highlights differences\n\n3. Interpret results:\n   - GREEN (Enforced): User B got different response (access denied) → SAFE\n   - RED (Bypassed): User B got same response as User A → VULNERABLE (IDOR)\n   - YELLOW: Needs manual review\n\n4. Review findings:\n   - Click on red entries\n   - See original User A request and replayed User B request\n   - Compare responses side-by-side\n   - Export for reporting\n\nENFORCEMENT DETECTOR CONFIG:\n```python\n# Example: Detect access denied\ndef is_enforced(response):\n    # Check for 403 status\n    if response.status_code == 403:\n        return True\n    \n    # Check for error message\n    if 'Access denied' in response.text:\n        return True\n    \n    # Check for redirect to login\n    if response.status_code == 302 and '/login' in response.headers.get('Location', ''):\n        return True\n    \n    return False\n```\n\nADVANCED FEATURES:\n1. Multiple unauthorized users:\n   - Test User B, User C, Admin against User A\n   - Detect vertical escalation too\n\n2. Custom headers:\n   - Include Authorization headers\n   - Test with different tokens\n\n3. Ignore list:\n   - Skip static resources (images, CSS, JS)\n   - Reduce noise\n\n4. Export results:\n   - CSV for spreadsheets\n   - HTML for reports\n   - JSON for integration\n\nLIMITATIONS:\n- Cannot understand business context\n- May flag legitimately shared resources\n- Requires manual setup of user accounts\n- Doesn't test complex multi-step flows\n\nBEST PRACTICES:\n1. Always test with at least 2 regular users and 1 admin\n2. Test all CRUD operations (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)\n3. Include API endpoints, not just web UI\n4. Test with different data types (orders, users, files)\n5. Document false positives for future reference\n6. Combine with manual testing for complex flows",
    "testing_tools", "hard", "A01-TOOL-102"
)

