add(
    "I found an IDOR vulnerability. How do I report it responsibly?",
    "Congratulations on finding an IDOR vulnerability! Here's how to report it responsibly:\n\nSTEP 1: DOCUMENT EVERYTHING\n1. Record the exact steps to reproduce:\n   - URL endpoint\n   - HTTP method (GET/POST/PUT/DELETE)\n   - Parameters modified\n   - Authentication state\n\n2. Capture proof:\n   - Screenshots of requests (Burp Repeater)\n   - Screenshots of responses\n   - Show User A accessing User B's data\n   - Include timestamps\n\n3. Assess impact:\n   - What data can be accessed?\n   - How many users affected?\n   - Can data be modified/deleted?\n   - Business impact assessment\n\nSTEP 2: FIND THE RIGHT CONTACT\n1. Check for security.txt:\n   - https://target.com/.well-known/security.txt\n   - Contains security contact and policy\n\n2. Look for bug bounty program:\n   - HackerOne, Bugcrowd, Intigriti\n   - Company's own program page\n\n3. Search for security email:\n   - security@company.com\n   - abuse@company.com\n\n4. Check CERT/CC database:\n   - For critical infrastructure\n\nSTEP 3: WRITE THE REPORT\nTemplate:\n```\nSubject: [Security] IDOR Vulnerability in [Product] - [Severity]\n\nVulnerability: Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR)\nSeverity: High (CVSS 7.5)\nAffected Endpoint: GET /api/users/{id}\n\nDescription:\nThe application does not verify that the authenticated user is \nauthorized to access the requested user object. An attacker can \naccess any user's profile by changing the user ID parameter.\n\nSteps to Reproduce:\n1. Log in as User A (user_id=123)\n2. Send GET request: /api/users/456\n3. Server returns User B's profile data\n4. Attacker can enumerate all user IDs\n\nImpact:\n- Access to PII of all users\n- Potential for mass data exfiltration\n- Compliance violations (GDPR, CCPA)\n\nProof of Concept:\n[Attach screenshots or curl commands]\n\nRemediation:\nImplement server-side ownership verification:\n- Filter queries by current user\n- Use authorization middleware\n- Return 403 for unauthorized access\n\nDisclosure Timeline:\nI request 90 days to allow for remediation before public disclosure.\n```\n\nSTEP 4: SUBMIT AND FOLLOW UP\n1. Submit via official channel (bug bounty platform preferred)\n2. Request acknowledgment of receipt\n3. Ask for timeline estimate\n4. Follow up every 2 weeks if no response\n5. Coordinate disclosure date\n\nSTEP 5: POST-REPORT\n1. Wait for fix before public disclosure\n2. Verify fix actually works\n3. Request CVE if applicable\n4. Write blog post (after disclosure)\n5. Update resume with finding\n\nETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS:\n- Never exploit beyond proof of concept\n- Don't access real user data unnecessarily\n- Don't share with unauthorized parties\n- Respect responsible disclosure timeline\n- Consider legal implications in your jurisdiction",
    "multi_turn", "hard", "A01-MT-101"
)

